Dialogue: 51,0:00:00.00,0:00:04.98,Notes,Pun/Wordplay,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}The name {\i0}Gintama{\i1} combines {\i0}"Gin"{\i1} (silver) and {\i0}"Tama"{\i1} ( soul), translating to "Silver Soul". However it is also a word play in that "Gintama" sounds awfully similar to "Kintama" which means testicles.
Dialogue: 50,0:00:00.00,0:00:04.98,Notes,,0,0,0,,{\an7\pos(0,0)\1c&HFFFFFF&\1a&H70&\blur0.5\p1}m 82 11 l 679 11 b 683.477 11 689 16.523 689 21 l 689 78 b 689 82.477 683.477 88 679 88 l 82 88 b 77.523 88 72 82.477 72 78 l 72 21 b 72 16.523 77.523 11 82 11
Dialogue: 51,0:00:18.04,0:00:20.04,Notes,Places,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}Lake Toya is a real lake in Japan where wooden swords engraved with "Lake Toya" are sold as souvenirs.
Dialogue: 50,0:00:18.04,0:00:20.04,Notes,,0,0,0,,{\an7\1c&HFFFFFF&\1a&H70&\blur0.5\p1\pos(-3.1,3.1)}m 158 12 l 603 12 b 607.477 12 613 17.523 613 22 l 613 58 b 613 62.477 607.477 68 603 68 l 158 68 b 153.523 68 148 62.477 148 58 l 148 22 b 148 17.523 153.523 12 158 12
Dialogue: 1,0:00:29.95,0:00:33.39,Notes,History,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}Japan's Sword Abolishment Edict (Haitōrei) of 1876 prohibited most people, including former samurai, from carrying swords in public, marking a major step in the dismantling of the samurai class and its traditional privileges.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.95,0:00:33.39,Notes,History,0,0,0,,{\an7\pos(0,0)\1c&HFFFFFF&\1a&H70&\blur0.5\p1}m 72 11 l 686 11 b 690.477 11 696 16.523 696 21 l 696 78 b 696 82.477 690.477 88 686 88 l 72 88 b 67.523 88 62 82.477 62 78 l 62 21 b 62 16.523 67.523 11 72 11
Dialogue: 51,0:00:33.51,0:00:43.40,Notes,Names/Folktales,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}Sakata Gintoki's name is based on Sakata Kintoki (also known as Kintaro), a legendary figure from Japanese folklore renowned for his incredible strength, who eventually became a samurai during the Heian Period.
Dialogue: 50,0:00:33.51,0:00:43.40,Notes,,0,0,0,,{\an7\pos(0,0)\1c&HFFFFFF&\1a&H70&\blur0.5\p1}m 82 11 l 678 11 b 682.477 11 688 16.523 688 21 l 688 78 b 688 82.477 682.477 88 678 88 l 82 88 b 77.523 88 72 82.477 72 78 l 72 21 b 72 16.523 77.523 11 82 11
Dialogue: 51,0:00:52.94,0:01:03.13,Notes,Names/Comedians,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}Shinpachi's first name is based on Shinsengumi captain Nagakura Shinpachi, who later went by Sugimura Yoshie (with Sugimura sounding similar to Shimura). His surname also references comedian Shimura Ken, reflecting his role as the series' straight man who delivers punchlines.
Dialogue: 50,0:00:52.94,0:01:03.13,Notes,,0,0,0,,{\an7\pos(0,0)\1c&HFFFFFF&\1a&H70&\blur0.5\p1}m 71 11 l 689 11 b 693.477 11 699 16.523 699 21 l 699 98 b 699 102.477 693.477 108 689 108 l 71 108 b 66.523 108 61 102.477 61 98 l 61 21 b 61 16.523 66.523 11 71 11
Dialogue: 51,0:01:36.70,0:01:45.25,Notes,Names/FolkTales,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}Kagura is based on Kaguya-hime from the Japanese folktale "The Tale of the Bamboo Cutter", in which the Moon-born princess eventually accepts her fate and returns to the Moon, leaving behind her loved ones on Earth. This connection is further reflected in Kagura's heritage as a member of the Yato (夜兎) clan, meaning "Moon Rabbit."
Dialogue: 50,0:01:36.70,0:01:45.25,Notes,,0,0,0,,{\an7\pos(0,0)\1c&HFFFFFF&\1a&H70&\blur0.5\p1}m 98 11 l 662 11 b 666.477 11 672 16.523 672 21 l 672 118 b 672 122.477 666.477 128 662 128 l 98 128 b 93.523 128 88 122.477 88 118 l 88 21 b 88 16.523 93.523 11 98 11
Dialogue: 51,0:02:04.27,0:02:10.18,Notes,TV Shows,0,0,0,,{\bord0\1c&H000000&\blur0.4}Ladies 4 was a long-running Japanese daytime television program also broadcast in TV Tokyo featuring lifestyle topics with Toshio Shiba as one of the presenters.
